This is what led us to the next step.

50
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:12,720
But essentially back.

51
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:52,400
in the day we were viewing them more as financial products and i think a lot of buyers at that price point think of apps as financial assets that deliver returns over x period of time yeah so in that case you're probably looking more for organic acquisition and when you say ebitta earnings for interest tax depreciation and amortization i forget all the all the parts of the long acronym so essentially if if you're spending 40k a month on user acquisition and you're making 40k a month you have zero ebitta you're not a good financial asset so what y'all were looking at was apps that were great at aso they had some kind of organic growth mechanism that was just spinning off cash.

52
00:04:52,560 --> 00:05:02,480
So if that's so if you've built a side project app and you're making 20 K a month in MRR and you don't have a bunch of expenses, that is attractive as a financial asset.

53
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:03,360
Literally.

54
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:03,600
Yeah.

55
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:06,080
It's exactly what you've what you just described there.

56
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:14,800
We should also kind of identify that the time period we're talking about, which is kind of three to four years ago, this whole trend of doing crazy organic on TikTok didn't exist.

57
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,160
So when we say TikTok, we're really talking about ASO keywords.

58
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:18,320
Yeah.

59
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:21,040
And if you picture it, it's really those apps that were the title.

60
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:25,520
So the apps that were called flashlight app or the apps that were called QR code scanner.

61
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:27,280
It's really trying to maximize that ASO.

62
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:27,760
Right.

63
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,200
And these days, a lot of people talk about organic TikTok.

64
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:35,280
as if it's free, but they're actually paying a ton of creators.

65
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:47,760
So similarly, if you're making 20k a month in MRR and you are spending 20k a month on organic TikTok, or that's your full-time job, it's like you're just churning out assets.

66
00:05:48,480 --> 00:06:00,560
If you're going to sell that to somebody with this more kind of financial asset approach, the buyer is going to say, oh, well, wait, you know, 40 hours a week of a great marketer's time, you know, creating these.

67
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:21,120
assets that go viral on tick tock or you know whatever kind of organic motion that you have if it's a high either cost because you're paying creators or a high time cost you know acquirers are generally going to look at that as a cost basis and not look at that kind of an app as a financial asset right Correct.

68
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:25,360
I think us in the industry labeling this TikTok strategy as organic is very misleading.

69
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:36,560
And even though you could pay five dollars for a single UGC video that shows off your app with a nice CTA at the end, and you may as well get a million, two million views, which may convert into a 10% conversion rate.

70
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:38,480
and you kind of get some downloads from that.

71
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:43,280
That being said, the time invested to get to that point is significant.

72
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:44,800
And I'm talking from experience.

73
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:47,440
We recently built this UGC machine at Bluethrone.

74
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:50,720
It took us like six months to get like our first viral video.

75
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:52,240
And that's not one person's time.

76
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:56,800
That's like multiple people across multiple disciplines doing the research, doing the execution.

77
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:59,440
So there is a cost associated to this organic.

78
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:09,280
But like all great mechanisms within the app business, you get better over time and you optimize and you're able to hit those targets faster with less resources as you get better, for sure.

79
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:12,720
So then what would your advice be to somebody in that position?

80
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:18,560
Either they're just getting started and they're thinking, hey, I'd love to build this app and exit it in 12 months.

81
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:19,840
I mean, I see this on Twitter all the time.

82
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:23,440
Like, I'm going to build this app and I'm going to sell it for 100k in a year.

83
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:31,840
What would your advice be to a founder like that today to kind of optimize that for a meaningful acquisition?

84
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:39,840
So if you're trying to optimize for a meaningful acquisition, let's say within a 12 month time frame, it's completely doable and we see it the whole time.

85
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,680
I think what you have to be is an absolutely killer marketer.

86
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:44,000
Right.

87
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:45,360
It's not about your dev skills.

88
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:47,360
And we talked about this over lunch on Friday, right?

89
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:55,600
In a time when anyone can build and the barriers to build are so low because of AI helping us here, the real differentiators are distribution.

90
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:08,880
And that's why we're seeing, you know, the people like the App Mafia guys and also a ton of other kind of people who don't make the spotlight as much, but really amazing marketers and distributors scaling the apps very, very quickly through TikTok, whether it's founder-led content.

91
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:14,240
So it's them telling their story, whether it's them interacting really healthily with influencers in their niche.

92
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:15,280
Let's take an example.

93
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:26,880
Let's say you've got a kind of Christian religion based app and you connect with the Christian religious influencers in that niche and you build relationships with them and they spin out content for you that can just shoot you up the charts very quickly as well.

94
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:32,640
And it's actually these guys that we're seeing get those exits within 12 to 18 months.

95
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:45,520
And from the buyer's perspective, like I think if I think about Bluethrone's perspective here, we'll always have concerns that an app that's only been around for 12 to 18 months has less historical data to put a value on top of it.

96
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:45,760
Right.

97
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:48,240
Because when you're valuing a business, you're looking at past performance.

98
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:58,000
That being said, that there are ways to do it because of the beauty of apps, which is you can measure attention and you can measure churn and you can measure repeated customers and you can measure conversion of free to paying users.

99
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:04,400
So you can pretty accurately predict how the app's going to do as long as you have maybe six to nine months of data from that app.

100
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:11,520
What would be the red flags then that you would advise folks to try and optimize away from?

101
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:24,560
That if you're thinking, okay, I see this app, you know, they sprung up from nothing, they're doing 50K in MRR, but what are the, when you're doing due diligence, what are the red flags you're looking for that say, oh, this is, this is either.

102
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:31,280
one, a much lower multiple that we're willing to pay or two, just like not an acquisition anybody would want to do.

103
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:32,640
It's a really good question.

104
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:36,880
Let's think about acquisition multiples on like a spectrum.

105
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:45,280
For those who aren't aware, a multiple is a number you'll apply to your annual revenue, which will give you the valuation and you can apply it to your revenue or your EBITDA.

106
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,600
But go look that up on YouTube and you'll learn a lot more.

107
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:57,360
Essentially multiples we can kind of view on a on a spectrum and at the lower end of the spectrum, you've got apps that are primarily doing revenue through advertising within the app, right?

108
00:09:57,520 --> 00:09:59,360
It's not very predictable as a buyer.

109
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:00,800
I cannot predict that into the future.

110
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:02,480
It also depends on your DAO and Mao.

111
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:04,880
So it's a little bit hard to put a true value on.

112
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:39,360
It also depends the demographic of your users because your ads are more valuable to tier one based users like in the US compared to tier three in India, for example right as you move towards the middle of the spectrum you've got ads that are monetizing through ads and iaps which is in-app purchases or one-time purchases now in-app purchases are great and i'm sure we'll get onto this in a minute but they are a little bit dangerous when it comes to valuing your app because it's not recurring revenue it doesn't hit that a r definition now on the far right of the spectrum to get the biggest valuation possible you're looking at apps that are pretty much 100% recurring revenue.

113
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:40,960
So subscription-based apps.

114
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:41,760
And why is that?

115
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:45,360
It's because as a buyer, your revenue is predictable.

116
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:53,200
If I have 12 months of data of your app, I know how many users are going to resubscribe when their yearly or monthly subscription is over.

117
00:10:53,600 --> 00:11:02,560
So I can predict the amount of money that your app is going to pull in into the future, which makes it really easy, whether it's me or anyone else, really to basically value the app and how much money it's going to generate.

118
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:07,440
And then you do that against the expenses generally.

119
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:15,200
So you're not, and I think that's something a lot of people get mixed up about when they're thinking, oh, my app's worth hundreds of thousands of dollars.

120
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:27,840
And then, you know, to your point earlier, if you're an AI app and you're spending like fifty percent of gross revenue on your AI costs, then you're going to get valued on the remaining 50%.

121
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:47,520
But if you're spending 50% on AI costs and you're spending 25% on marketing and you're spending 20% on salaries that would need to continue in the business as an attractive acquisition, it's looking less and less attractive as those costs go up higher and higher.

122
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:57,360
And then the other thing I'll say, and I was just tweeting about this yesterday, is that what most people in our industry call MRR and ARR are not actually MRR and ARR.

123
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:09,040
So I'll get on my soapbox for one sec and say monthly recurring revenue, annual recurring revenue, you know, in RevenueCat, we have this great chart now.

124
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:13,200
And then I've been lobbying to create some additional charts around this is that...

125
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:18,800
You know, once somebody's turned off that auto renew very early in the cycle.

126
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:28,560
And we have some data at RevenueCat that actually shows the highest month of people turning off auto renew in an annual subscription is actually the first month.

127
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:38,880
And so you can get pretty predictive of what revenue is actually going to recur if you know that first month of people turning it off and then fit the curve.

128
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:51,120
And so when you look at your dashboard and it says, you know, 100k in MRR or, you know, a million dollars in ARR, it's not ARR, it's ARR.

129
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:57,360
And then to get to ARR, you really need to discount by like the known churn stats.

130
00:12:57,760 --> 00:13:17,760
And whether that's, you know, predicting into the future with that first month or whether that's you have a year or two of data where you can look back and see what those cohorts are retaining like, you need to discount that by the known churn or even the estimated churn to get a true ARR and a true MRR.

131
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:24,400
Because again, I just think it's in consumer where the, you know, median retention of an annual plan is like 35 percent.

132
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:26,000
It's not AR.

133
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:27,280
It's just not.

134
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:36,960
That brings me on to like a really interesting point if we if we draw this back to the kind of valuation topic, which is so often you get a founder come and say, hey, I've got X amount of subscribers on the yearly subscription.

135
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:38,720
And it's recurring because it's yearly.

136
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:43,280
But the killer question is like, how many people are going to come back and pay at the end of that time period?

137
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:49,440
And if your app has not been around for twelve months, you cannot tell me your resubscriber rate.

138
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:50,400
You just can't.

139
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:51,600
But, but you can estimate it.

140
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:51,840
Yeah.

141
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:52,800
You can estimate it.

142
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:56,880
Uh, but you can't tell me for a fact how many users are actually going to come back and and pay that money again.

143
00:13:57,360 --> 00:13:58,080
Yeah, totally.

144
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:00,720
So a high churn rate would be one of those red flags.

145
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:03,360
And yeah, a high churn rate would be one of those red flags.

146
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:05,360
Let's do four more and let's just kind of list them off.

147
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:08,000
So listeners have like a five-point checklist, right?

148
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:11,920
So top one is churn rate because that's where your subscription revenue is coming in.

149
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:15,200
And it, the churn rate kind of dictates how stable that revenue is.

150
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:21,040
It's also a great indicator of like how effective your product solves the problem it's set up to solve, which is awesome.

151
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,640
Then obviously your whole CAC to LTV ratios are like super, super important.

152
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:27,200
Is the way you're buying traffic profitable?

153
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:30,320
Does it have viral potential or is it capped?

154
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:35,280
So basically knowing where the tip of your RAS curve is going to be and how far you've been able to push that in terms of budget.

155
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:36,320
That's number two.

156
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:40,000
And number three is basically talent, team composition, who's in the team.

157
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:47,520
You know, I'm seeing so many incredible solo founders who want to build stuff and basically hit that kind of million dollar exit within a year.

158
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:48,560
Super doable.

159
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:49,920
We've seen it happen time and time again.

160
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:51,520
A lot of deals that we've been a part of.

161
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:56,000
However, there can be some confusion when people say, hey, I'm a solo founder.

162
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:56,720
Amazing.

163
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,400
But how many consultants are you using?

164
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:00,160
How many outsourcers are you using?

165
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:01,680
Completely fine to use them.

166
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:06,800
Everyone does and it's a really cost effective way of scaling up your business, but just great to be honest about it.

167
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:19,280
Hey, I've got X number of devs in, I don't know, Brazil, Turkey or India, and I've got a marketing guy in California, just so we know that even though you are a solo founder and it's just your equity, there are other players involved and the buyer will need to take account of that.

168
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:20,240
That's number three.

169
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:24,480
Number four is all this research around the kind of category you're playing in.

170
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:31,280
It's a very different app, whether it's playing in education, fitness, photo and editing, or if it is indeed like a QR code scanner.

171
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:32,640
The competition matters.

172
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:34,480
How well funded is the competition?

173
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:37,360
How aggressive is the competition on UA, for example.

174
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,600
So that's super, super important.

175
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:44,240
And the fifth point I will say has to be whether you've raised money or not.

176
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:49,840
If you've raised money from VCs, then your valuation will be inflated because you have to keep your VCs happy.

177
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:55,840
If you haven't raised money from VCs and maybe you're bootstrapped or you've done a friends and family round, the power is all your.

178
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:03,600
You can sell at a lower valuation, but you're keeping a lot more money to take home and start your next project with or retire your parents or buy a house or whatever it might be.

179
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:12,880
But I want to throw this to you, David, because it's something we talk about a lot at Bluetooth, which is, do you think it makes sense for consumer apps to raise VC money in this day and age?

180
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:14,240
I think it can.

181
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:22,160
And I was actually thinking about that on the drive here of what kinds of apps are truly investable in 2025.

182
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:37,360
And, you know, looking back at my six years at RevenueCat, you know, I've talked to hundreds, hundreds of founders and app practitioners and, you know, all sorts of folks in my office hours at conferences at, you know, random meetings I end up getting booked.

183
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:40,560
And I'll say I feel like I was a little naive.

