00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:28,960
It can often be a great outcome.

376
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:35,040
The only downside to those outcomes is they'll look for a pretty big earnout because they spot you as a talent and they want to keep you internally.

377
00:32:35,600 --> 00:32:36,400
So that's one.

378
00:32:36,960 --> 00:32:44,720
Second type of buyer is more of a private equity focused buyer who are basically looking for profits or EBITDA, they want to see really, really great margins.

379
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:47,840
These guys, the upside is there's a lot around.

380
00:32:48,560 --> 00:32:52,320
The downside is they're going to want you to stay on board because they don't have the team to manage your app.

381
00:32:52,640 --> 00:32:53,600
They need you to manage your app.

382
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:56,480
And the other downside is they're going to optimize for like EBITDA.

383
00:32:56,640 --> 00:33:00,400
So it might get a little messy, not naming any names, but they generally like to cut the team.

384
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:06,880
Then your third piece of the puzzle is these kind of like app acquirers, similar to Bluetooth, and there's a bunch of other in the industry.

385
00:33:07,200 --> 00:33:09,040
I'm not going to do them any favors and shout them out.

386
00:33:09,200 --> 00:33:09,840
Sorry guys.

387
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:14,800
But essentially people like us, we want to buy the app for its future growth potential.

388
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:19,440
And the benefit there is that the founder doesn't need to stay on for a super long period of time.

389
00:33:19,600 --> 00:33:22,400
It's often a very, very short handover with a lot of cash in advance.

390
00:33:22,480 --> 00:33:23,760
So you kind of take the money and walk away.

391
00:33:24,240 --> 00:33:29,600
And companies like this will try and take that app to be a category leader and hold it there for as long as possible.

392
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:32,640
The first deal we did was in was in the music entertainment space.

393
00:33:32,880 --> 00:33:34,000
It was a solo founder.

394
00:33:34,640 --> 00:33:36,240
We paid him basically seven figures.

395
00:33:37,360 --> 00:33:40,400
deal for an app he'd spent two years building and the deal was closed within two months.

396
00:33:40,640 --> 00:33:41,760
That was almost four years ago.

397
00:33:41,840 --> 00:33:44,000
We bought it at about 200 K MAU.

398
00:33:44,880 --> 00:33:46,560
And since then, we added to the team.

399
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:50,480
We hired a killer GM from Spotify actually to manage this music app.

400
00:33:50,800 --> 00:33:56,320
We grew the team from five to fifteen people and we grew the MAU from 200 K to almost six million today.

401
00:33:56,560 --> 00:33:58,240
And it's still pretty much top of the category.

402
00:33:58,400 --> 00:34:01,200
When I'm talking to a found and they ask for a case study, I always give that one.

403
00:34:01,360 --> 00:34:09,520
But actually, because we're judged on our track record, we have to be very strict about the deals we do because one bad deal can kind of torpedo the whole company.

404
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,560
So actually, our track record is great for now.

405
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:17,440
But with MA and this kind of playbook, you know, one bad deal can really mess things up for good.

406
00:34:18,160 --> 00:34:23,760
So in that third category that you were just mentioning that you lumped Blue Throne into, there is a very wide spectrum.

407
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:30,640
And so I'd say there is the Blue Throne one point zero of buying a ton of apps looking at more of a financial instrument.

408
00:34:31,440 --> 00:34:33,520
So tell me about Blue Throne two point zero though.

409
00:34:33,680 --> 00:34:35,600
So Blue Throne two point zero, it's a great question.

410
00:34:35,760 --> 00:34:41,600
Essentially, we learned our lesson that buying this kind of wide, diverse portfolio of apps doesn't really work because you're buying financial instruments.

411
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:49,120
And at the end of the day, we were apps guys and we knew apps and our team was made up of guys from mobile gaming like myself, from ad tech and from consumer techTech.

412
00:34:49,440 --> 00:34:58,800
So Bluethrone 2.0 is really about finding apps that have found product market fit that have a lot of organic traffic, which is a great signal for us because it means no one is being paid to download your app.

413
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:04,000
They found it out of word of mouth or k factor or just searching for something on the App Store themselves.

414
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:13,600
And these product market fit apps have enabled themselves to rise to mid or top of their category by providing a deep product experience that solves the issue they set out to solve.

415
00:35:14,400 --> 00:35:15,120
That's the first thing.

416
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:24,720
And we realized that if we acquired these and actually invested in them and the team, we could keep them as category leaders for as long as possible and really turn them into like standalone companies.

417
00:35:25,200 --> 00:35:28,000
And so all the apps we buy have like a significant brand image.

418
00:35:28,320 --> 00:35:29,680
They have really strong organic traffic.

419
00:35:29,840 --> 00:35:33,760
They have great retention, great resubscriber rates and of course like a killer founding team.

420
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:36,800
Now sometimes the founding team wants to stay and that's great.

421
00:35:36,880 --> 00:35:37,200
We love that.

422
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:39,040
We've had people stay with us for like three years.

423
00:35:39,440 --> 00:35:43,520
Sometimes they want to take their money, buy a house, buy a car, pay off their parents' house and go.

424
00:35:43,680 --> 00:35:44,560
That's also fine with us.

425
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:47,600
The whole deal structure is about finding what works for both parties.

426
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:51,920
But that strategy we've been employing for about two and a half years now.

427
00:35:52,240 --> 00:35:54,480
And it means our portfolio is much smaller.

428
00:35:54,560 --> 00:35:56,240
We now have a portfolio of five apps.

429
00:35:56,720 --> 00:35:57,520
I saw your face here.

430
00:35:57,680 --> 00:35:58,560
I looked a little surprised there.

431
00:35:58,720 --> 00:35:59,040
It's true.

432
00:35:59,200 --> 00:36:04,080
It's only five apps, but each app is a either a product leader in its category or on its way to be.

433
00:36:04,800 --> 00:36:07,600
It's a completely different strategy, but luckily, it's working well for us now.

434
00:36:07,840 --> 00:36:10,320
I've talked to the founders of Blue Throne a few times over the years.

435
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:16,000
And so I knew Blue Throne as the old strategy where there was like 100 plus apps in the portfolio.

436
00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:24,480
And so when you told me that Friday and then when you told me again today, it is surprising because it's not what I remember and think of Blue Throne as.

437
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:28,400
But it's obviously a whole different ball game.

438
00:36:29,200 --> 00:36:34,320
And I mean personally, you know, if I were in the app acquisition space, this is the approach I would be more taking.

439
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:36,240
Which Blue Throne two or Blue Throne one?

440
00:36:36,400 --> 00:36:37,360
Blueluetooth phone two.

441
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:37,520
Yeah.

442
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:38,800
I'll ask you a question.

443
00:36:38,960 --> 00:36:41,280
If you could buy any app, what would you buy as David?

444
00:36:41,840 --> 00:36:43,600
Oh, that is a really good question.

445
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:53,680
Off the top of my head, I would say my friend Ryan Jones's app flighty because it's just one of those kind of category leaders with so many opportunities.

446
00:36:54,240 --> 00:36:56,240
I don't know that 50 million would do it at this point.

447
00:36:56,480 --> 00:36:57,280
He's crushing it.

448
00:36:59,280 --> 00:37:00,480
But, you know, high retention.

449
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:09,920
you know, high willingness to pay, a lot of like ancillary opportunities in the travel space to partner with companies.

450
00:37:10,160 --> 00:37:12,720
So like there's obvious like future B2B play.

451
00:37:13,680 --> 00:37:15,360
He has done almost no paid acquisitions.

452
00:37:15,520 --> 00:37:16,960
So it's a very organic growth.

453
00:37:17,520 --> 00:37:21,200
I think my dream app is probably Opal, the screen time app, for a few reasons.

454
00:37:21,200 --> 00:37:22,880
One is they raised VC though.

455
00:37:23,120 --> 00:37:25,600
They did raise VC, so I'll be paying a pretty penny for it.

456
00:37:25,760 --> 00:37:29,360
I don't know if I can afford it, but I love this app because the design is incredible.

457
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:31,840
It looks like it was built by Apple and Cupertino itself.

458
00:37:32,320 --> 00:37:35,840
The problem it's solving is super significant, basically helping people stay off their phones.

459
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:38,640
And they've unlocked this crazy LTV.

460
00:37:38,880 --> 00:37:40,320
You know what you want they charge yearly?

461
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:42,320
120, 120 bucks per year.

462
00:37:42,640 --> 00:37:46,480
And the whole logic I've listened to a bunch of interviews by Kenneth, the founder is super smart.

463
00:37:46,880 --> 00:37:48,640
The whole logic is they target knowledge.

464
00:37:49,040 --> 00:37:56,560
workers and they say, if I can reduce your screen time by fifty percent a week, you'll be making more money in your, in your probably high paying job.

465
00:37:57,040 --> 00:38:02,560
And that more money you're making is well worth more than the 120 bucks a year you're going to be spending on my app.

466
00:38:02,800 --> 00:38:04,160
So you're better off spending the money.

467
00:38:04,480 --> 00:38:07,680
And it works, it's a fantastic business that solves a real problem.

468
00:38:08,080 --> 00:38:12,480
So Kenneth, if you're interested, come find me, but I'm also not sure I can afford you.

469
00:38:12,640 --> 00:38:13,520
But yeah, shout out.

470
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:14,720
Well, I love Opal too.

471
00:38:14,880 --> 00:38:16,160
I've had Kenneth on the podcast.

472
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:19,600
Actually, he just emailed me this morning about our app growth annual conference.

473
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:23,520
So this is not to take away from Opal, but let's let's play it out.

474
00:38:24,240 --> 00:38:32,880
some of the red flags, some of the challenges in the category, because Opel and other apps have done well, there's now a proliferation of these apps.

475
00:38:33,040 --> 00:38:35,040
And so there's a ton of competition in the space.

476
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:40,320
How have you been thinking about a space like screen time management?

477
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:59,840
I assume I'm going to put words in your mouth here, but I assume it's that even in these spaces, there will be the kind of category winner so that even if there are 100 apps doing similar things, if you can buy the category leader, they're going to continue to thrive regardless of what the competition is.

478
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:11,120
But you wouldn't want, based on Blue Throne 2.0's positioning, you wouldn't want to buy one of the 100 other apps to go compete with Opel.

479
00:39:11,760 --> 00:39:18,480
And you've probably looked at some of them because they're so clearly breaking away from the crowd as the category leader.

480
00:39:18,960 --> 00:39:20,000
Is that how you think about this?

481
00:39:20,320 --> 00:39:24,320
So you're a bit unlucky here because I actually did analysis on this the other week.

482
00:39:24,480 --> 00:39:25,920
So I'm going to hit you with some data here.

483
00:39:26,400 --> 00:39:35,840
Screen time is a really great category because if you do a, if you go on Sense Tower and you do a cross section of downloads over time and you stack them, there's a great viewing way of doing this on Sense Tower.

484
00:39:36,160 --> 00:39:49,920
You can see that not only is the category growing over time, which is great ever since Apple allowed you to use the API to build these apps, but what you can see is that if you cross section it by downloads, every time a new app enters the market, the whole market grows.

485
00:39:50,480 --> 00:40:03,840
And to me as an investor or a buyer, this tells me there are users that the market leader has not tapped into yet, that if a new entrance comes in and has a different value proposition or branding or appeal, they can actually capture the capture those users.

486
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:05,200
So that's really interesting.

487
00:40:05,280 --> 00:40:06,480
And the same thing happens with the revenue.

488
00:40:06,560 --> 00:40:18,880
If you cross section this category by revenue, you'll also see that every time a new company pops in, there's been one from YC and a couple of others, they're able to capture market share without taking it from Opel, even though Opel is clearly the category leader.

489
00:40:19,120 --> 00:40:25,920
So in this niche in particular, I would feel okay with buying something that isn't a category leader because I think there's space for it to grow.

490
00:40:26,320 --> 00:40:28,960
You've also got to remember that screen time is like a pretty new category.

491
00:40:29,120 --> 00:40:29,200
Yeah.

492
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:32,240
Weather apps, QR code scanners, they've been around for decades.

493
00:40:32,960 --> 00:40:40,160
Screen time has only been around for like less than ten years, probably more like six since Apple got the API, which brings me to another topic of like Sherlocking.

494
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:44,560
You know, some might say, well, hey, Apple is providing the service organically on the phones anyway.

495
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:45,920
So why would you pay $120?

496
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:53,920
And to that, I'd say, hey, if you offer a better experience with an improved UI UX, maybe some social features, you've actually got space to grow and that's been proven already.

497
00:40:54,240 --> 00:40:55,600
And I was laughing.

498
00:40:55,840 --> 00:41:07,920
The camera would probably be focused on you during that answer, but I was sitting across from you laughing because as a parent, I can tell you that their screen time functionality sucks.

499
00:41:08,560 --> 00:41:12,560
And I actually was just talking on Twitter with Kenneth, the founder of Opel.

500
00:41:12,960 --> 00:41:24,800
He said this publicly on Twitter that they're moving that direction and wanted to like do a user interview with me of like all the problems I have managing my family screen time with Apple's stuff.

501
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:34,480
And that's the thing about Sherlocking is like Apple does create great products generally, but any one of their products is going to have so many holes in it.

502
00:41:34,720 --> 00:41:37,680
And then specifically, some of their products just aren't great.

503
00:41:37,840 --> 00:41:41,440
And I would love their screen time product into that.

504
00:41:42,160 --> 00:41:47,680
There is a challenge there that as they make it better, and this is an interesting one, you know, I have a weather app.

505
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:50,560
And historically, Apple's weather app has been crap.

506
00:41:50,720 --> 00:41:52,160
Well, they actually did invest quite a bit.

507
00:41:52,320 --> 00:41:53,280
They bought Dark Sky.

508
00:41:53,920 --> 00:41:58,880
They invested a lot in the user experience and created a much better default experience weather app.

509
00:41:59,360 --> 00:42:00,560
Now, did that take.

510
00:42:01,120 --> 00:42:02,720
away from the rest of the weather app market?

511
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:04,240
I actually don't think it did.

512
00:42:04,560 --> 00:42:11,280
Like, I probably should sit down and do a much deeper analysis like you've done, but there is always a potential that they will improve the product over time.

513
00:42:12,320 --> 00:42:16,640
And it could cut into the opportunity for that market to continue to grow the way it has.

514
00:42:17,280 --> 00:42:19,200
But historically, that just has not been the case.

515
00:42:19,760 --> 00:42:24,480
Fitness, they came out with Fitness Plus and it's bundled into the Apple One subscription.

516
00:42:25,040 --> 00:42:33,040
And yet we see Runna and Ladder and so many fitness apps like zero to one since Apple launched Fitness Plus doing incredibly well.

517
00:42:33,280 --> 00:42:43,120
So yeah, I don't think SureLocking and Apple providing it at the system level or even as a add-on like fitness plus should dissuade you from playing in the category.

518
00:42:43,280 --> 00:42:51,520
In fact, maybe that's a sign that it's a good category to play in and you go create that better user experience and serve the different use cases that Apple's never going to serve.

519
00:42:51,680 --> 00:42:52,000
Absolutely.

520
00:42:52,160 --> 00:42:54,480
Apple, for them, it's a question of focus and ROI.

521
00:42:54,640 --> 00:42:59,120
And if they, the behemoth that they are, turn their attention to something that you're looking at, you're probably on the right track.

522
00:42:59,280 --> 00:42:59,840
Yeah, totally.

523
00:43:00,480 --> 00:43:00,720
All right.

524
00:43:00,880 --> 00:43:05,520
Well, I did want to dive into another topic and we've done so many tangents.

525
00:43:05,840 --> 00:43:09,040
We're not even going to cover probably half the topics we were planning on covering today.

526
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:17,280
But I did want to talk about increasing LTV and kind of breaking through some of the ceilings.

527
00:43:17,520 --> 00:43:20,000
It's something I've been talking a ton about, hybrid monetization.

528
00:43:20,720 --> 00:43:30,800
We've seen early leaders in the subscription space like Tinder, you know, find ways to better monetize through multi-tier subscriptions, through consumables, through one-time purchases.

529
00:43:31,680 --> 00:43:34,560
How do you think about the opportunities ahead?

530
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:36,720
Because I think we're super early.

531
00:43:37,120 --> 00:43:43,520
And I love that you come from gaming because I feel like gaming is perpetually five or ten years ahead of the rest of the software makers.

532
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:52,000
So how do you think about hybrid monetization and ways to increase LTV not just by increasing subscription price.

533
00:43:52,240 --> 00:43:59,040
Consumer apps on a subscription basis typically have maybe four price points, which is your weekly, your monthly, your yearly and your lifetime.

534
00:43:59,280 --> 00:44:11,760
And if you imagine a graph with your X and Y axis and a kind of steps or stairs going up to the middle, you're able to capture user willingness to spend at four different points, the price point of your weekly, monthly, annual and lifetime.

535
00:44:12,480 --> 00:44:14,400
Now let me put a question to you app founders out there.

536
00:44:14,560 --> 00:44:22,640
Let's say you're charging ten bucks for your monthly and you're charging one hundred for your yearly, but you've got a user that's actually willing to spend seventy five.

537
00:44:23,680 --> 00:45:06,880
How do you capture that value he's not going to pay a hundred he might pay ten but because he was willing to spend 75 you're missing 65 worth of value now gaming cracked this a long time ago because gaming works on this curve that shoots up to the right and to the top of the graph because gaming uses gatcha mechanics and in-app purchases one-time purchases for example so if a gatcha box costs you 99 cents and you're willing to spend 10 you'll make that purchase 10 times and the app has been able to capture all of the money you were willing to spend so how can you capture this from consumer subscription apps well the solution we came to on Friday, which is of no surprise to anyone, I'm sure, is introducing in-app purchases, you know, onetime purchases into your app.

538
00:45:07,120 --> 00:45:07,760
And let's give an example.

539
00:45:08,080 --> 00:45:10,480
There's an amazing app called SoundMap, for example.

540
00:45:10,960 --> 00:45:14,560
SoundMap, I spoke to the founder a while ago now, a great guy called Zibo.

541
00:45:15,120 --> 00:45:16,960
SoundMap is Pokemon Go for music.

542
00:45:17,120 --> 00:45:25,760
So you'll walk around your city using your phone and you'll find tracks in your city and you'll collect that music track, whether it's like a Drake song, and then you'll be able to trade that with someone else.

543
00:45:25,920 --> 00:45:27,760
Maybe they've got a Metallica song and you can trade that.

544
00:45:27,920 --> 00:45:29,520
So there's peer to peer sharing there as well.

545
00:45:29,680 --> 00:45:40,240
Now they have a subscription which is capturing the majority of the value of the users, but they also have consumables and power-ups that, for example, let you discover two times more songs in your area.

546
00:45:40,800 --> 00:45:44,240
So they're able to capture all the value of the user that they're willing to spend.

547
00:45:44,800 --> 00:45:50,000
And I think the issue here for a lot of consumer apps is that they don't all lend themselves to this kind of monetization.

548
00:45:51,360 --> 00:46:03,600
But the real unlock we've seen from Blue Thrones level, talking to, you know, hundreds of app founders a month, is the ones that can crack this generally can create deeper LTVs, which gives you way more breathing room on your CPIs and your marketing budget.

549
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:04,080
Yeah.

550
00:46:04,720 --> 00:46:11,360
I think this is such an underexplored space so far in the non-gaming app world.

551
00:46:12,640 --> 00:46:17,760
And, you know, consumable IAP in some ways is probably the holy grail.

552
00:46:18,000 --> 00:46:19,280
And this is what we see with Tinder.

553
00:46:19,600 --> 00:46:20,640
And Tinder is a perfect example.

554
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:25,520
One of the things we talked about over lunch was this famous graph from Ravi Meta, famous to me.

555
00:46:26,000 --> 00:46:29,840
I just think not everyone is as deep into this as you are.

556
00:46:31,120 --> 00:46:35,280
But it's looking at that willingness to number of users across the horizontal axis.

557
00:46:35,840 --> 00:46:39,600
And then in the vertical axis, that willingness to pay and amount spend.

558
00:46:40,320 --> 00:47:01,120
And what's so beautiful about Tinder's model is that they have subscription tier, a lower subscription tier that can capture way more users at that lower price and they have a mid-tier subscription where it's a higher price but fewer users and then they have the highest paid subscription tier which is going to be the highest LTV subscription but the lowest number of users.

559
00:47:01,760 --> 00:47:11,360
But then layered on top of those boxes is those consumables where you can just perfectly match that demand curve all the way up and down.

560
00:47:11,680 --> 00:47:12,560
And so that is...

561
00:47:13,600 --> 00:47:17,200
optimal, but to your point, like not every app can find that mechanic.

562
00:47:17,760 --> 00:47:22,400
I would say look for it, like figure out if there's a way to do that.

563
00:47:23,040 --> 00:47:27,120
But if you don't, there's a ton of other hybrid monetization opportunities out there.

564
00:47:27,680 --> 00:47:38,080
And one that I think is still underexplored in subscription apps is ads and even rewarded ads is something I've hardly seen in subscription apps.

565
00:47:38,960 --> 00:47:57,360
But it's one of those things where, you know, maybe if you're a scanner app and somebody's got zero willingness to pay, but they just need that one scan, maybe you do a rewarded ad and or you do an app purchase, you know, three bucks just for one scan and then they can move on with their life.

566
00:47:57,840 --> 00:48:01,200
Now, for most apps and scanners are probably a good example of this.

567
00:48:01,440 --> 00:48:08,240
You probably are going to be a higher LTV, charging them on a weekly subscription and a certain amount of people forget or whatever.

568
00:48:08,800 --> 00:48:14,000
